# core.autocrlf=true (repo default) corrupted models/classifier.txt on
# checkout: it's a plain-text LightGBM model dump, git's heuristics see
# "text" and apply LF->CRLF conversion, which mangled the line-based
# format badly enough that LightGBM's parser read garbage ("Model format
# error, expect a tree here" on load) -- even though the committed blob
# itself was byte-for-byte fine (confirmed via `git show <rev>:<path>`,
# which bypasses checkout filters). Marked -text/binary below so this
# can't happen again, for anyone on the team, on any future clone or
# checkout -- not just a local workaround. See STATUS.md.
models/classifier.txt -text
models/dataset_train.csv -text
models/dataset_holdout.csv -text
*.wav binary
*.npy binary

# Same hole, second time. The rules above cover *.wav, *.npy and the three
# models/ files; they do not cover *.bin, and zoo/corpus/ccsds/ ships eight
# .payload.bin. `git check-attr text binary` reported "unspecified" on all of
# them, so git falls back to its content heuristic -- and these files are pure
# ASCII with zero NUL bytes, so the heuristic calls them text.
#
# Nothing is corrupted today, and only by luck: they also contain zero newline
# bytes, so LF->CRLF is a no-op on the current corpus. But `payload_text` is
# caller-supplied, so the first payload containing a newline gets mangled on
# every Windows checkout -- exactly the failure models/classifier.txt already
# cost us. Closing it before that payload exists rather than after.
*.bin binary
